  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nUpper level ridge axis moves into the Great Lakes for the end of the \nweek as high pressure settles into the region at the surface. The \nupper trough that has moved east of the area continues to bring lake \nenhanced cloud cover ashore in breezy northerly flow, and should end \nafter 12Z this morning except for NW PA where some cloud cover could \nhang on into the afternoon aided by mixing. Expecting mostly clear \nconditions today otherwise in a cooler and slightly lower dewpoint \nairmass. This airmass will modify each day with slightly higher \ntemperatures into the low to mid 80s by Friday. Meanwhile, Thursday \nwill be sunny once again while Friday has increasing cirrus \ncloud cover from the west. \n\n \nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nAn upper level trough will track into the southern Great Lakes from \nthe upper Mississippi Valley while generating surface low pressure \nover the mid Mississippi Valley. An inverted surface trough from the \nsurface low will set up over the CWA for Saturday and Sunday, \nbringing back higher dewpoints, a return of low/mid level moisture, \nand patchy, weak instability in a pattern that will not progress\nmuch during that period. In the end, expecting temperatures to \nremain in the low to mid 80s for the weekend and chance POPs \nwith some uncertainties in the coverage and timing.",
